  "guide": {
    "bricks": "A brick is bike-straight-into-run with no rest. Your legs feel like concrete for the first 5–10 min off the bike — that's normal, and training it is the #1 thing that makes 70.3 race day feel manageable. Run the brick portions at race effort (Z2→Z3), short at first, building to 25 min.",
    "swim": "You're already a comfortable swimmer, so the swim build is about (1) reaching a smooth continuous 2,000m and (2) open-water reps — sighting, wetsuit feel, and staying calm in a crowd. Get at least 3–4 open-water swims in from Week 5.",
    "bike": "The bike is your biggest build and the leg where the race is won or lost (~3 hrs, longest leg). The long Saturday ride is the anchor session every week — protect it. Practice race nutrition on every long ride.",
    "fuel": "On the bike, take in 60–90g carb/hr and sip electrolytes — the bike is where you fuel for the run. Rehearse it in the Week 8 race-sim so nothing's new on race day.",
    "strength": "2×/wk maintained through the build (Mon power/lower doubles as your dunk base; Fri upper + calisthenics). Cut to light in the taper. Never fully drop — it's your knee insurance.",
    "knee": "Run volume is kept moderate (long run caps ~11mi) on purpose — the bike/swim carry the aerobic load so the knee stays healthy. Any flare: pull a run before a bike or swim. The durability staples stay in."
